[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said one issue, one fix queued. Its own cause table shows a second
cause, and its footnote holds a third. Each needs its own fix, so each has its own ticket.
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed and booking-v3 is retired. The third
problem is still open.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

| problem | ticket | state |
|---|---|---|
| `parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first | [#1](https://github.com/zalio-pl/mega-support-kb/issues/1) | fixed |
| `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type `MM/DD/YYYY` | [#2](https://github.com/zalio-pl/mega-support-kb/issues/2) | won't fix: booking-v3 retired |
| one-day move: dates shift a day earlier across time zones | [#3](https://github.com/zalio-pl/mega-support-kb/issues/3) | open |

<Callout kind="warn">
Closing #4471 did not close the one-day move. Those reports are filed under <code>parser</code> by
tenant and only footnoted, so the parser fix hid them. They keep coming until a time-zone fix
ships.
</Callout>

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: every tenant writes the day first and the parser reads the month first. Day and
  month swap: 7 April arrives as 4 July.
- **`hint`**: people type month first, as the booking-v3 hint tells them, and the form reads it
  day first. It looks like the parser bug, but the fix belongs in the form.
- **one-day move**: no swap. Tuesday shows as Monday; Lisbon gets Tuesday and Toronto Monday. Most
  likely a time zone or DST conversion.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone. All three
problems produce the same subjects, so subject matching cannot keep them apart. Splitting them
needs the tenant, the channel and the body.

